WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.484
Read the change in source before applying
it. We return to the

00:00:03.484 --> 00:00:07.193
same sum of squares operation that we
tested and measured earlier.

00:00:07.324 --> 00:00:10.339
The proposal avoids an intermediate array
by using

00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:13.113
a lazy reduction with an explicit
initializer.

00:00:13.244 --> 00:00:17.039
That initializer matters for empty input,
but the entire rewrite

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:20.597
still needs validation for the package's
types and numerical

00:00:20.597 --> 00:00:24.274
semantics. Check what changed and what the
agent left assumed.

00:00:24.405 --> 00:00:27.679
Does the operation preserve the intended
result? Are the

00:00:27.679 --> 00:00:31.012
representative inputs covered? Does a
numerical reduction

00:00:31.012 --> 00:00:33.994
require a tolerance or preserve a
particular order?

00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:38.438
Are side effects or error cases part of
the public behavior? Inspect

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:42.878
the proposed test and any actual oracle
output from the isolated copy.

00:00:42.878 --> 00:00:47.001
Passing one example cannot establish
equivalence for every input.

00:00:47.132 --> 00:00:50.691
The purpose of this review is to decide
whether the proposal and

00:00:50.691 --> 00:00:54.750
validation are adequate for your package.
Apply remains a human decision.
